---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import '../styles/landing.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith('/') ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
const repo = 'https://github.com/ydah/flexr';
---
<SiteLayout
  title="Predictable lexers, written in Ruby"
  description="Write lexer specifications in Ruby, inspect longest-match decisions, and generate deterministic Ruby for deployment with flexr."
>
  <main class="landing">
    <section class="landing-hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <div class="hero-intro">
        <p class="hero-kicker">A Ruby-native lexer generator</p>
        <h1 id="hero-title">Turn Ruby rules into predictable tokens.</h1>
        <p class="hero-summary">
          Write one readable specification. Run it directly while you iterate, inspect why each
          match wins, then generate deterministic Ruby when it is time to ship.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="landing-button primary" href={`${base}learn/getting-started/`}>Build your first lexer</a>
          <a class="landing-button secondary" href={`${base}playground/`}>Explore the matching model</a>
        </div>
        <dl class="hero-facts">
          <div><dt>Runs on</dt><dd>Ruby 3.1+</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Ships as</dt><dd>Runtime or generated Ruby</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>

      <figure class="lexer-map">
        <figcaption>
          <span>lexer.flexr.rb</span>
          <span>longest match</span>
        </figcaption>
        <pre><code><span class="syntax-keyword">class</span> Lexer &lt; Flexr::Lexer
  rule(<span class="syntax-regexp">/==/</span>) &#123; emit <span class="syntax-token">:EQ</span> &#125;
  rule(<span class="syntax-regexp">/=/</span>)  &#123; emit <span class="syntax-token">:ASSIGN</span> &#125;
  rule(<span class="syntax-regexp">/if/</span>) &#123; emit <span class="syntax-token">:IF</span> &#125;
  rule(<span class="syntax-regexp">/[a-z_][a-z0-9_]*/</span>) &#123; emit <span class="syntax-token">:IDENT</span> &#125;
<span class="syntax-keyword">end</span></code></pre>
        <div class="scan" aria-label="Input if equals equals total becomes IF, EQ, and IDENT tokens">
          <div class="scan-input" aria-hidden="true">
            <span>if</span><span class="space">·</span><span class="active">==</span><span class="space">·</span><span>total</span>
          </div>
          <div class="scan-tokens">
            <span><small>if</small>IF</span>
            <span class="active"><small>==</small>EQ</span>
            <span><small>total</small>IDENT</span>
          </div>
          <p><code>/==/</code> wins over <code>/=/</code> because it consumes more input.</p>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-section workflow" aria-labelledby="workflow-title">
      <header class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="workflow-title">A lexer you can reason about before it reaches your parser.</h2>
        <p>flexr keeps the specification, the matching contract, and the deployed scanner connected.</p>
      </header>
      <ol class="workflow-list">
        <li>
          <span class="step-number">1</span>
          <div><h3>Write ordinary Ruby</h3><p>Define rules, states, token values, and actions in a class that fits beside the rest of your application.</p></div>
          <code>class Lexer &lt; Flexr::Lexer</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number">2</span>
          <div><h3>Inspect the decision</h3><p>Longest match wins; source order resolves ties. Diagnostics catch unsupported patterns before deployment.</p></div>
          <code>flexr explain lexer.flexr.rb</code>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="step-number">3</span>
          <div><h3>Generate when needed</h3><p>Produce deterministic Ruby and verify that runtime and generated scanners emit the same token stream.</p></div>
          <code>flexr generate lexer.flexr.rb</code>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-section modes" aria-labelledby="modes-title">
      <header class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="modes-title">Keep one source of truth.</h2>
        <p>Choose the deployment boundary without rewriting the lexer in a second language.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mode-grid">
        <article>
          <h3>Runtime mode</h3>
          <p>Load the specification directly for a tight development loop, tests, and applications that prefer the readable source.</p>
          <ul><li>No generation step</li><li>Immediate rule changes</li><li>Full Ruby action context</li></ul>
          <a href={`${base}learn/runtime-mode/`}>Read the runtime guide</a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Generated mode</h3>
          <p>Compile the same specification into deterministic Ruby for reproducible builds and controlled startup behavior.</p>
          <ul><li>Reviewable generated artifact</li><li>Standalone output available</li><li>Runtime parity checks</li></ul>
          <a href={`${base}learn/generation/`}>Read the generation guide</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="parser-strip" aria-label="Parser integrations and project properties">
      <p>Built for parser authors</p>
      <ul><li>Racc</li><li>Lrama</li><li>Custom parsers</li><li>Unicode-aware</li><li>Open source</li></ul>
    </section>

    <section class="landing-cta" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="cta-title">Start with a lexer you can read.</h2>
        <p>Install the gem, define your first rules, and see the token stream in a few minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <code>gem install flexr</code>
        <a class="landing-button light" href={`${base}learn/getting-started/`}>Open the quick start</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="landing-footer">
      <a class="footer-brand" href={base}>flexr/</a>
      <nav aria-label="Footer navigation">
        <a href={`${base}examples/`}>Examples</a>
        <a href={`${base}reference/cli/`}>CLI reference</a>
        <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>
      </nav>
      <p>MIT licensed. Built for Ruby parser authors.</p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
